#: includes/Security/Findings.php:151
msgid "A file that comes with WordPress isn’t there, so parts of the dashboard may fail, and it can be a sign that files were tampered with."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Security/Findings.php:151
msgid "Dashboard → Updates → Re-install version puts it back."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Security/Findings.php:152
msgid "An extra file among WordPress’s own"
msgstr ""

#: includes/Security/Findings.php:152
msgid "WordPress never adds files to wp-admin or wp-includes outside updates, so hackers hide backdoors there."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Security/Findings.php:152
msgid "If nobody on your team put it there, delete it, then re-install WordPress from Dashboard → Updates and change your passwords."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Security/Findings.php:153
msgid "An extra PHP file in the site’s main folder"
msgstr ""

#: includes/Security/Findings.php:153
msgid "Besides WordPress’s own files and wp-config.php, PHP files here are usually left-over tools or a hacker’s backdoor."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Security/Findings.php:153
msgid "If you don’t know what it’s for, ask your developer or host, then delete it."
msgstr ""

